<worksheet>
    <title>Homework 03</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            <alert>MTH 124</alert>
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <p>
        <alert>Due Thursday, 9/24/26 at 12:00pm (the beginning of class)</alert>
    </p>

    <p>
        Make sure you are following the homework expectations!
        Example: complete sentences!
    </p>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                (This activity is adapted from Dan Meyer, who worked for Amplify (formerly Desmos)!) To start, go to <url href="http://gvsu.edu/s/2zf">gvsu.edu/s/2zf</url> where the following data points are plotted
	            <md>(.87, 7.92), \,\, (1.74,9.2),\,\,  (2.69,10.44),\,\,  (3.68,11.5), \,\, (4.65,12.42), \,\, (5.64,13.24)</md>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                   Find the slope between the first and last point given above.  (Complete sentences, for example ``The slope between the first and last points is ....")
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use the slope from part (a) and the first data point to find an equation for a line that approximates the data points. (You can plot this line in Desmos to check your work.)  Write a sentence about whether or not this line seems to be a good fit. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    This data is actually from a stop motion shot of a basketball which you can watch the first second of the video here: <url href="http://gvsu.edu/s/2zg">gvsu.edu/s/2zg</url>. Why does a linear function not make sense as a model?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Make a prediction: does the ball go in or out?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Here's a still of the basketball video plotted on the graph with the data points from the beginning of this problem.
                </p>
            <image width="60%" source="images/Problem3-Basketball.png"/>
            <p>
                Estimate the last point from the axis overlaying the picture, and assume that's the vertex.  Use that and one other data point to write the equation of a quadratic modeling the basketball. (Vertex form: <m>y=a(x-h)^2+k</m>.)  What data point did you use?  (Explain!)
            </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Plot your equation from the previous part. Estimate a point for the basket using the picture, and use your quadratic model to determine if the ball goes in the basket. (Does the quadratic you graphed go through that point roughly?) 
                </p>

            
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Spoiler: here's the video of the whole shot - <url href="http://gvsu.edu/s/2zh">gvsu.edu/s/2zh</url>. Were you right? (If you want to mess around with a bunch of these you can go <url href="https://gvsu.edu/s/3NB">gvsu.edu/s/3NB</url>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>


    </exercise>
</worksheet>